From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LeavesMC <noreply@leavesmc.org>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 10:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Command NBT selector optimizations

Bellows speeds up the two hot paths of datapack-heavy servers: the NBT tree
that `/data` has to serialise for an entity, and the whole-dimension scan that
`@e` performs.

For the NBT side this port does NOT copy vanilla's per-field `save`/`load`
dispatch, which is what the upstream mod has to do. Upstream needs 18 mixins to
clone `saveWithoutId`/`load` field by field, because the loader offers no hook;
here the sources are available, so `EntityDataAccessor#getData` instead runs the
real `Entity#saveWithoutId` through a `FilteringValueOutput` view that lets only
the top-level keys the datapack asked for reach the `TagValueOutput`. One wrapper
replaces 18 mixins and can never drift from upstream save logic.

Only the pure-read paths are routed through it: `data get`, `data get <path>`,
the source side of `data modify`, `execute if data`, and the `nbt=` predicate of
selectors and advancements. The read-modify-write paths (`data modify`,
`data remove`, `data merge`, `execute store ... data`) deliberately keep the
vanilla full serialisation, because `DataAccessor#setData` feeds `Entity#load`,
which resets every key absent from the tag (`Pos` falls back to zero, `Tags` is
cleared, `data` is reset to empty). Filtering the read side there would teleport
entities to 0,0,0 and wipe their tags and custom data, so those call sites are
left byte-for-byte vanilla.

For the selector side, the parser records the positive `type=`/`tag=` constraints
of each selector into `SelectorHints`, and `EntitySelector#addEntities` may then
resolve `@e` against a type/tag inverted index maintained by the entity lookup,
instead of walking every entity of the dimension. The hints are recovered by
scanning the raw selector text, so no `lambda$bootStrap$NN` ordinal anchors are
involved. Moonrise's `EntityLookup` is the lookup actually used on this branch,
so the index hangs off it, and every candidate is re-checked against
`EntityLookup.getEntityStatus(...).isAccessible()` before it reaches the
predicate; hidden and already-removed entities therefore cannot leak into
results. Indexing is skipped whenever the selector carries an explicit limit,
because then the result set is order-sensitive.

Everything is gated behind three `leaves.yml` options that default to off; with
them disabled every injected line falls through to the original code.

This patch is Powered by Bellows(https://github.com/Smithed-MC/Bellows)

